I'd worked across six
different continents.

00:14:51.723 --> 00:14:53.896
I'm never going to go to Antarctica,
right?

00:14:53.896 --> 00:14:57.605
[LAUGH] But that opportunity,
I'm still African at heart.

00:14:57.605 --> 00:14:59.810
That opportunity opened my mind,

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:05.200
because I started realizing that there's
different ways of leading, right?

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.198
And right now the premise
of how we lead and

00:15:07.198 --> 00:15:09.920
the whole education system to be honest,
right?

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:13.843
And even the workplace, etc,
it's influenced by one system of thinking,

00:15:13.843 --> 00:15:15.830
one system of thoughts, right?

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:18.851
And what I really appreciated
about being at Stanford is that it

00:15:18.851 --> 00:15:22.737
really opened our minds a lot to the fact
that there's more to the world, right?

00:15:22.737 --> 00:15:24.994
And there's also a lot
more places to learn from.

00:15:24.994 --> 00:15:28.525
So we learned from whether it's
the first or developing world and

00:15:28.525 --> 00:15:30.380
all kinds of for the world.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:33.341
So then when you're talking about
leadership, I think for me having come

00:15:33.341 --> 00:15:36.412
back to the African continent,
I've learned and I've realized, right?

00:15:36.412 --> 00:15:40.196
I mean, I mentioned that I spend my days a
lot with multinationals, the CEOs of these

00:15:40.196 --> 00:15:43.800
big giants, right, helping them
make these businesses sustainable.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:48.023
And one of the things that encouraged
them is it's really important to lead

00:15:48.023 --> 00:15:50.520
from what is authentic to you as a leader.

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:51.282
Right?

00:15:51.282 --> 00:15:54.009
Because you cannot be someone else,
you can't try and

00:15:54.009 --> 00:15:56.440
fit into someone else's scripts, right?

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:58.510
That's not who you are,
that's not your core.

00:15:58.510 --> 00:16:01.659
And one of the things about us being
from the African continent as some

00:16:01.659 --> 00:16:05.460
of the people that are listening is that
there's a certain ethos of leadership that

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:07.850
is part of our DNA from so
many generations.

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:09.580
That's what the world needs right now.

00:16:09.580 --> 00:16:11.255
So how do we leverage that, right?

00:16:11.255 --> 00:16:14.549
For all of us, as other leaders in
different parts of the world do the same

00:16:14.549 --> 00:16:18.070
because then what ends up happening is
the world benefits from that, right?

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:19.740
And that's what I enabled leaders to do.

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:23.338
Even the leaders that I work with
that are not from South Africa, or

00:16:23.338 --> 00:16:25.985
from different parts of
the African continent.

00:16:25.985 --> 00:16:29.249
And they're coming here to have impact and
drive businesses, and

00:16:29.249 --> 00:16:31.356
I say to them this them there's a premise.

00:16:31.356 --> 00:16:35.193
How do you think about bringing what you
know, but how do you think about honoring

00:16:35.193 --> 00:16:37.912
the best of what comes from
while you are here basically?

00:16:37.912 --> 00:16:40.663
But if you ask me,
whether it's self awareness, right?

00:16:40.663 --> 00:16:44.338
Or that level of that ability to
challenge yourself and say, but

00:16:44.338 --> 00:16:47.342
what is authentic and
what is real to you as a leader?

00:16:47.342 --> 00:16:52.713
It really comes from how I was challenged
in my own journey in the MBA, right?

00:16:52.713 --> 00:16:56.166
As much as I was happy with my
progress and my achievements and

00:16:56.166 --> 00:17:00.297
I was keen to learn more, there I was
really challenged to really grow and

00:17:00.297 --> 00:17:02.790
ask myself what kind of leader am I?

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:06.455
And then what does that look like when
you then go out to the world, right?

00:17:06.455 --> 00:17:09.366
Which is what I did, and
also what I loved and

00:17:09.366 --> 00:17:14.910
I reflect back fondly on is that I was
never encouraged to follow any scripts.

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:16.400
I was really encouraged.

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:20.170
I feel like for me whenever I reflect
about my journey with Stanford,

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:24.510
they encouraged me to be the best
version of myself that I could be.

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.740
Whatever that is, right?

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:29.507
And stuff, and so today yes,
I've always been entrepreneurial.

00:17:29.507 --> 00:17:33.923
I mean since I was five years old I was
running businesses for survival, for

00:17:33.923 --> 00:17:36.530
sustenance, then fun, then everything.

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:38.829
And then yesterday I had businesses but

00:17:38.829 --> 00:17:43.576
it's something that I'm realizing now
what kind of intrapreneur I am, right?

00:17:43.576 --> 00:17:46.728
And stuff, and then also having
the opportunity to create jobs and

00:17:46.728 --> 00:17:49.610
really create opportunities for
young people to work with.

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:53.302
Think about it, in what space do you get
an opportunity to get access to leaders of

00:17:53.302 --> 00:17:54.567
multinationals, right?

00:17:54.567 --> 00:17:57.890
And stuff, and so being able to create
that opportunity for young talent is

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:02.250
something for me that's been important and
then that's been really, really critical.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:06.342
And I think the last one thing that I'll
also mention is that one of the benefits

00:18:06.342 --> 00:18:09.394
of also just seeing different
parts of the world, right?

00:18:09.394 --> 00:18:14.175
Is I think it really helped me
appreciate the strengths, right?

00:18:14.175 --> 00:18:18.390
Appreciate and honor the strengths
of my journey, where I come from,

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:21.495
because sometimes people
tell half the story.

00:18:21.495 --> 00:18:25.205
We talk about the things,
the struggle, ladies and get them out.

00:18:25.205 --> 00:18:29.495
But there's lessons from the difficulty
and there's lessons from that growth.

00:18:29.495 --> 00:18:30.805
And let's be honest, right?

00:18:30.805 --> 00:18:32.420
Look at what happened to 2020.

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:34.870
It put trauma in all of us.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:37.941
Everybody, everybody, and
we had to ask ourselves,

00:18:37.941 --> 00:18:41.870
where have you faced difficulty,
where have you faced struggle?

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:44.956
And guess what, we know the scripts
we have the answers just because of

00:18:44.956 --> 00:18:48.010
the legacy of where we come from
as a continent, as a people.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:51.321
This is the time that the world needs to
learn from us and I'm very passionate

00:18:51.321 --> 00:18:54.601
about just making sure that that's
taken to the rest of the world, right?

00:18:54.601 --> 00:18:56.390
As much as possible.

00:18:56.390 --> 00:19:01.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I love the way
you talk about being empowered

00:19:01.510 --> 00:19:05.849
to embrace kind of what is there and
share it and

00:19:05.849 --> 00:19:10.762
like find that strength in
that place of who you are.

00:19:10.762 --> 00:19:12.567
So that's amazing.

00:19:12.567 --> 00:19:18.410
Kind of bridging from something you
said before this is for Julian Eugene.

00:19:18.410 --> 00:19:22.661
She was talking about like challenging
the way that you see yourself,

00:19:22.661 --> 00:19:25.600
challenging the way you see the world.

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:29.761
Kind of on the threshold of your
GSB experience as you're returning

00:19:29.761 --> 00:19:34.141
to the continent, can you talk a little
bit about maybe an experience or

00:19:34.141 --> 00:19:36.780
class that has challenged you in that way?

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:41.565
I know there's a couple of questions
in the Q&amp;A box about were there any

00:19:41.565 --> 00:19:43.241
classes in particular,

00:19:43.241 --> 00:19:49.220
what were some of the things that helped
you see kind of yourself in new ways?

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:53.498
So maybe Zwelihle we can start with you
and then Eugene will follow with you,

00:19:53.498 --> 00:19:54.924
&gt;&gt; Sean, [COUGH] now thanks for

00:19:54.924 --> 00:19:56.030
the question.

00:19:56.030 --> 00:20:00.741
So for me, I think a lot of it was
trying to explore what it meant very

00:20:00.741 --> 00:20:06.060
experientially to be an entrepreneur
in startup business, right?

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:10.515
So my prior background was working
in consulting that in industry,

00:20:10.515 --> 00:20:16.223
consumer telecoms, I'd always started
things like organizations in high schools,

00:20:16.223 --> 00:20:18.030
smaller businesses.

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:23.224
But I would have never used the term
entrepreneur to define myself at all and

00:20:23.224 --> 00:20:25.350
not to kind of show why.

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:29.077
But I think the idea of then now
having basically uninhibited

00:20:29.077 --> 00:20:33.715
what does it look like to start
something from absolutely nothing.

00:20:33.715 --> 00:20:37.291
So concepts interviews, trying to
interview users in South Africa in

00:20:37.291 --> 00:20:41.473
the value recruits people and put things
together made me realize how much of those

00:20:41.473 --> 00:20:45.670
skills I might have learned, even though
I wouldn't have fully appreciated it.

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:49.886
And therefore how much of that can be
a very big part of like the career and

00:20:49.886 --> 00:20:51.580
choices that I've chosen.

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:53.539
And I think that it would
have been very hard for

00:20:53.539 --> 00:20:55.900
me to make that transition
outside of that.

00:20:55.900 --> 00:21:00.396
So it is both the classes, but
also the idea that there's this

00:21:00.396 --> 00:21:05.333
big theme at GSB around doing
something that really matters to you.

00:21:05.333 --> 00:21:07.257
But also challenging yourself to say,

00:21:07.257 --> 00:21:10.534
what does it look like when you
start this from zero to one, right?

00:21:10.534 --> 00:21:14.448
So my prized business consulting was
like helping big companies grow and

00:21:14.448 --> 00:21:17.219
there's a certain skill and
thinking set, yeah.

00:21:17.219 --> 00:21:21.791
But that idea of training across kind
of people management, marketing,

00:21:21.791 --> 00:21:25.859
functional areas, zero to one for
me was very kind of impactful.

00:21:25.859 --> 00:21:29.667
And that was both in the classes but
also in the experiences interacting

00:21:29.667 --> 00:21:33.231
judge the classmates,
the kinds of speakers that come to school.

00:21:33.231 --> 00:21:37.485
And that had a big, big impact, very
direct impact which just made me choose

00:21:37.485 --> 00:21:40.963
a different career choice
immediately during the pandemic.

00:21:40.963 --> 00:21:43.580
But that was like a big impact for me.

00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:45.780
&gt;&gt; Yes, and then Eugene,
how about yourself?

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:49.364
Like what are some of the ways that GSB
has helped you challenge kind of the way

00:21:49.364 --> 00:21:50.820
you see yourself in the world?

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:53.596
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Will.

00:21:53.596 --> 00:21:55.460
I think I'll speak to two things.

00:21:55.460 --> 00:22:00.165
I think the first really transformative
thing that happened to me at

00:22:00.165 --> 00:22:03.980
the GSB was I learned
how to embrace failure.

00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:08.691
Because I think one of the things MBA does
really, the program really stretches you,

00:22:08.691 --> 00:22:10.598
I think beyond your imagination.

00:22:10.598 --> 00:22:13.433
And there's so
many opportunities in an MBA program,

00:22:13.433 --> 00:22:15.165
you think you can do everything.

00:22:15.165 --> 00:22:18.980
So I remember I really
loaded up my summer.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:23.300
Last summer I wanted to do two,
three internships and travel the world and

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:25.752
go explore the startup space in Nigeria.

00:22:25.752 --> 00:22:29.677
And I remember my last assignment
I just wasn't able to complete it,

00:22:29.677 --> 00:22:32.731
I was just too stretched and
I didn't know what to do.

00:22:32.731 --> 00:22:36.173
And I just reached out to
the Stanford C team that was

00:22:36.173 --> 00:22:38.746
working on that internship program.

00:22:38.746 --> 00:22:40.146
And I told them you know what,

00:22:40.146 --> 00:22:44.170
I don't think I'll be able to complete
my tasks in this last assignment.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:46.416
And I think for
me the reaction that is cool,

00:22:46.416 --> 00:22:49.699
because I initially thought I'm
going to get punished for these,

00:22:49.699 --> 00:22:52.270
I committed to something
that I can't complete.

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:54.559
But I think the way
the school reacted to that,

00:22:54.559 --> 00:22:56.978
they may even have put me
in touch with an alumni.

00:22:56.978 --> 00:23:01.388
And I got a lot of help around that in
terms of accepting when you feel you've

00:23:01.388 --> 00:23:05.600
taken on too much and accepting
failure and actually learning from it.

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:08.589
And I remember one thing this
particular alumni told me and

00:23:08.589 --> 00:23:12.330
he's actually one of the biggest
investors in the continent.

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:15.420
He told me, he doesn't invest in
anyone who has never made a mistake.

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:17.475
And I was like,
mistakes are part of learning.

00:23:17.475 --> 00:23:19.946
And thus that is not something I hadn't,
you come to GSB,

00:23:19.946 --> 00:23:24.230
maybe you're a bit of a perfectionist,
you're used to getting everything, right?

00:23:24.230 --> 00:23:28.359
And I think for me, this was one time that
I realized I can't always be right and I

00:23:28.359 --> 00:23:33.470
can't always do everything by myself, and
I should be open to reaching out for help.

00:23:33.470 --> 00:23:36.730
And I'd say the other thing
is taking a bit more risk.

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:38.386
So going to business school initially,

00:23:38.386 --> 00:23:41.306
imagine I want to go to this school,
and then come back and work for one of

00:23:41.306 --> 00:23:45.220
the biggest investors in the African
continent, the more established names.

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:49.021
And then you go to Stanford and you're
exposed of both types of businesses and

00:23:49.021 --> 00:23:52.700
you realize how this is a lot
more exciting to work with.

00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:57.990
Sort of smaller new businesses that are
still experimenting their business model.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:01.560
And so that's how I ended up deciding to
go to Africa 50 which is barely four years

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:05.110
old and just try to figure out their
strategy around the continent.

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:06.800
And I'm testing a lot of new things.

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:09.258
So it feels even though I'm working for
a big firm,

00:24:09.258 --> 00:24:14.080
it still feels very startup issue, I think
that's something I picked up the GSB.

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:18.856
And eventually how I see that panning out
is I'd probably be trying to do a fund in

00:24:18.856 --> 00:24:20.800
a couple of years down the line.

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:24.883
&gt;&gt; And
then Eugene you mentioned Stanford seed,

00:24:24.883 --> 00:24:27.970
can you tell us a little bit
more about that group, please?

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:30.350
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Stanford seed for

00:24:30.350 --> 00:24:33.394
me was actually one of the reasons I
was really keen about going to Stanford

00:24:33.394 --> 00:24:37.240
especially because I knew I want to
come back to the continent right after.

00:24:37.240 --> 00:24:41.773
So this is an entrepreneurship initiative
that supports a close to 1,000

00:24:41.773 --> 00:24:43.960
businesses now in Africa.

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:48.614
And so Stanford seed takes African
entrepreneurs through an entrepreneurship

00:24:48.614 --> 00:24:51.887
training program to help
them scale their businesses.

00:24:51.887 --> 00:24:56.108
And they do allow Stanford students
both from the business school as well as

00:24:56.108 --> 00:25:00.880
other schools to intern at Stanford Seed,
and Stanford pretty much meets the cost.

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:03.639
So it's a it's actually a really
good way for you to try and

00:25:03.639 --> 00:25:06.454
learn about a part of the continent
that you haven't been,

00:25:06.454 --> 00:25:09.270
because many of us grew up in our,
South Africa.

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:13.047
And that's all you know about Africa,
maybe you know your neighboring countries.

00:25:13.047 --> 00:25:16.790
But maybe you've never worked to lived in
North Africa and Stanford seed gives you

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:20.332
that opportunity as an MBA student about
your first and your second summers.

00:25:20.332 --> 00:25:24.520
You could actually do two different
internships in different parts of Africa.

00:25:24.520 --> 00:25:28.300
And get to sort of increase your knowledge
and network about the continent.

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:33.000
So for me that was one of the best things
about Stanford for an African student and

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:37.492
also a very strong partner for the Africa
business club in terms of funding,

00:25:37.492 --> 00:25:40.340
helping out with events and networks.

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:45.237
So it really truly makes experience for
an African a bit more wholesome.

00:25:45.237 --> 00:25:47.678
&gt;&gt; Africa business club you just said,

00:25:47.678 --> 00:25:53.330
it is selfishly one of my favorite groups,
they have such amazing people.

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:57.849
And we were on a panel recently,
and they're talking about how,

00:25:57.849 --> 00:26:03.292
this just the celebration of what it meant
to be African at the GSB at Stanford.

00:26:03.292 --> 00:26:08.075
And just the way the energy of all
the panelists lit up was amazing,

00:26:08.075 --> 00:26:12.355
and I know we have a folks here
who were involved in the club.

00:26:13.745 --> 00:26:17.556
I'll open this up to whoever would
someone like to talk a little bit about

00:26:17.556 --> 00:26:18.895
Africa business club?

00:26:18.895 --> 00:26:23.680
What it does, maybe any experiences
that just really spoke to you from it?

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:27.411
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I can go, because, I mean,
now I'm seeing how dated I am but

00:26:27.411 --> 00:26:31.995
just to give you a context Africa students
in the jesting wasn't that high when I was

00:26:31.995 --> 00:26:33.250
there, right?

00:26:33.250 --> 00:26:36.074
And I guess some questions
here were people asking,

00:26:36.074 --> 00:26:39.100
what is the Stanford alumni
network done for you, etc.

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:42.868
That wasn't a big, to be honest that
was a big decision I had to make.

00:26:42.868 --> 00:26:44.435
Do I go to the West Coast or

00:26:44.435 --> 00:26:49.241
some of the East Coast schools around
who's got the better Africa network?

00:26:49.241 --> 00:26:52.150
When I go to Stanford, and
I was joining the clubs,

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:56.619
I joined the black Business Association
as well as the ABC, but they were not,

00:26:56.619 --> 00:26:59.349
ABC wasn't that big was
kind of seen as sports.

00:26:59.349 --> 00:27:02.969
But then I've seen it over the years,
particularly after I left.

00:27:02.969 --> 00:27:06.154
So this is kind of the back question
of what has the GSB done for

00:27:06.154 --> 00:27:08.170
me after I've left?

00:27:08.170 --> 00:27:11.423
I almost feel I've got so
much out of it the last at least five or

00:27:11.423 --> 00:27:15.428
six years that I did even that, because
of course when you graduate from the GSB,

00:27:15.428 --> 00:27:18.470
those first three years
depends what you're doing.

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:19.945
But I was always at Microsoft and

00:27:19.945 --> 00:27:22.680
trying to find my feet,
I wasn't going back to Africa yet.

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:25.272
Not in Africa, as I mentioned.

00:27:25.272 --> 00:27:30.232
But looking back on it one of
the things that has happened is,

00:27:30.232 --> 00:27:35.410
I've had two GSB case studies,
done going after businesses.

00:27:35.410 --> 00:27:38.931
Go links of big finance and
the Bitcoin crypto space,

00:27:38.931 --> 00:27:42.860
that I kicked that was written up in 2014,
right?

00:27:42.860 --> 00:27:47.193
So I know crypto hot now or
kind of was hot last year this year, but

00:27:47.193 --> 00:27:52.169
it was also hot in the previous wave 2014,
15, so I had a GSB case and

00:27:52.169 --> 00:27:54.850
it was the first crypto case in the GSB.

00:27:54.850 --> 00:27:59.220
And it was also one of the first
African startup cases, right?

00:27:59.220 --> 00:28:05.815
And then more recently, I think it is
2017,18 airobotics a drone company,

00:28:05.815 --> 00:28:11.934
and as I used to work in aerospace from
a bio, so I was an ag tech drone company,

00:28:11.934 --> 00:28:17.800
both African cases but also there was
a class has become more popular now.

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:21.472
I don't know how it is with in
recent years the [INAUDIBLE] and

00:28:21.472 --> 00:28:26.990
a few other professors are doing the
merchant rockets entrepreneurship class.

00:28:26.990 --> 00:28:31.345
These case studies and there was an
emerging markets entrepreneurship class,

00:28:31.345 --> 00:28:35.050
when I was there, but I've gone to
co teach those classes right, or

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:38.567
guest lecture, which I love with
my own case studies of course.

